mp has  shown to be a great odor mitigator  it's highly absorbent without the odors  similar to hay but just a little bit  more effective so there's studies around  that as well um i think probably in the  next couple years we'll start to see  some  provisional licenses or research  companies coming out with different  innovative ways of applying it what  considerations would you suggest with  regards to gmp standards for facility  design  so again i'm a lab rat so i'm always  going to say high quality  high consideration to safety so when it  comes to gmp standards i definitely  think that um if you can afford to  outfit your facility to meet the highest  standards whether that's you know if  you're in the states 210 to 11  if you're in you know europe it's eugmp  but um if you can meet the highest level  of standard i think you should go for  that every time because again um just  kind of taking off the hat of  entrepreneur capitalist you have to  think uh this is a product that i'm  putting into the market for potentially  children to consume or you know  immunocompromise elderly people to  consume so you do have to have a bit of  a conscience there  could you talk about baseline cost  approximations uh comparing different  extraction methods uh kind of generally  what would be more or less expensive in  regards to equipment  sure  um so i i've done a presentation before  uh speaking about extraction um  equipment and what the pricing has been  and i can say just historically uh when  you're looking at high capex costs  usually your supercritical fluid co2 is  going to be at the top of the list um  one of the reasons is behind you know  the pumps that are needed uh the  condensers that are needed to recycle  the solvent you have just the pure scale  of a lot of the co2 instruments and the  pressure uh ratings that you're using  when you're in service so the the safety  implications there's a lot there  that you have to consider so that's  usually the highest cost